// Signing and identity for the sample app.
//
// A Team ID is not a secret in the cryptographic sense — it ships inside every
// distributed binary — but it identifies a person while the account is personal,
// and a repository that will be public should not carry it. So this file holds
// the parts that are the same for everyone, and the optional include at the
// bottom picks up whatever is local to you.
//
//   cp Local.xcconfig.example Local.xcconfig   # then fill in your Team ID
//   xcodegen generate
//
// Local.xcconfig is gitignored. Without it the project still generates and still
// builds to a simulator; only device deployment needs a team.

// Reverse-DNS of the platform hostname, which is `cloud.moqom.example` — the
// same name `dns_domain` carries in deploy/envs/*/terraform.tfvars.example, so
// the two cannot drift apart.
//
// Still a placeholder, and the `.example` is why: RFC 2606 reserves that TLD for
// documentation, so it can never be registered and will never resolve. Nothing
// here breaks on that — a bundle identifier is just a unique string and Apple
// does not check DNS — but a Cloud DNS zone for it would serve records no client
// on the internet can look up. Change both places together when the real domain
// exists.
MOQOM_BUNDLE_PREFIX = example.moqom.cloud

PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER = $(MOQOM_BUNDLE_PREFIX).room

// Automatic signing suits a personal team and a sample. The organisation
// account, when it arrives, will want manual signing with an explicit profile;
// that is a change to Local.xcconfig and this line, and nothing else.
CODE_SIGN_STYLE = Automatic

// Empty is a valid value: it builds for the simulator and fails only when you
// ask for a device, which is the correct place to discover you have not set it.
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =

// Last include wins, so anything below overrides everything above. The `?`
// makes it optional — a fresh clone with no local file generates fine.
#include? "Local.xcconfig"
